{"answer":"There are 7 such subsets. One example is {1,8}. Exhaustively: a subset summing to 9 has size 2 or 3, since no singleton is available and four distinct positive elements sum to at least 1+2+3+4=10. The 2-element subsets are {1,8}, {2,7}, {3,6}, {4,5}; the 3-element subsets are {1,2,6}, {1,3,5}, {2,3,4}. Thus the total is 4+3=7.","approaches":[{"description":"Classify subsets by cardinality and enumerate increasing pairs and triples.","id":"cardinality-enumeration","outcome":"candidate","reason":"The minimum-sum bound excludes sizes at least four, leaving finitely many pairs and triples to enumerate."}],"change_log":[],"claims":[{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived by the cardinality bounds and the pair/triple enumerations in the proof steps.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"seven-subsets","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Exactly 7 subsets of {1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8} have sum 9.","step_ids":["size-bounds","pairs","triples","total"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Exhibited directly in the proof.","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":["seven-subsets"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"example-subset","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"{1,8} is a subset with sum 9.","step_ids":["pairs"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"size-bounds","justification":"The empty subset has sum 0; a singleton subset would have to be {9}, which is unavailable; and any subset with at least four distinct positive elements has sum at least 1+2+3+4=10. Hence only sizes 2 and 3 are possible.","statement":"Any subset of the given set with sum 9 has cardinality 2 or 3."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["size-bounds"],"id":"pairs","justification":"For an increasing pair {a,b}, a+b=9 and a<b imply a=1,2,3,4, yielding respectively b=8,7,6,5.","statement":"The sum-9 pairs are {1,8}, {2,7}, {3,6}, and {4,5}."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["size-bounds"],"id":"triples","justification":"For an increasing triple {a,b,c}, a+b+c=9. If a=1, then b=2 or 3 yields c=6 or 5; larger b gives c<=b. If a=2, then b=3 yields c=4; larger b gives c<=b. If a>=3, then a+b+c>=3+4+5=12. These are all possibilities.","statement":"The sum-9 triples are {1,2,6}, {1,3,5}, and {2,3,4}."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["pairs","triples"],"id":"total","justification":"There are four listed pairs and three listed triples, and their cardinalities differ, so the lists are disjoint.","statement":"The exhaustive total is 4+3=7."}],"question_status":"answered","tool_requests":[]}
